From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lukasz Sobala <lsobala1@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 00:52:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: cvitek: always power and mux the SD slot on probe

cvi_sdhci_probe() checks card detect before setting up the SD slot. With a
card present it switches the slot's power on at 3.3V and muxes
SD0_CLK/CMD/D0-D3 to the SDIO0 function; with the slot empty it does the
opposite - power switch open and discharging (TOP_SD_PWRSW_CTRL = 0xE), bus
pads muxed away to XGPIO, pull-downs applied.

For the vendor stack that is harmless: their kernel driver repeats the same
pad-and-power dance on every card-detect interrupt. Mainline Linux does not.
The sdhci0 node has no vmmc regulator and no mainline driver knows the power
switch register, so whichever state U-Boot leaves is the state the system
keeps. Booting from eMMC with the slot empty therefore kills SD hotplug for
the whole Linux run - inserting a card later produces not one dmesg line,
since the card sits unpowered in a slot whose bus pads are disconnected from
the controller.

Take the card-present path unconditionally. That reproduces exactly the
state of a boot with a card in the slot, where hotplug (remove and
re-insert, mmc0 in the boot log) is verified to work, and keeping VDD on an
empty slot is what ordinary SD hosts do anyway.

sdhci-cv180x.c carries the same logic, but this family builds only cv181x
boards, so it is left alone.
---
 drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c | 29 ++++++++++++-----------------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c b/drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c
index 09edc9a..75d7da3 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/cvitek/sdhci-cv181x.c
@@ -642,23 +642,18 @@ static int cvi_sdhci_probe(struct udevice *dev)
 		sdhci_writel(host, sdhci_readl(host, CVI_SDHCI_VENDOR_MSHC_CTRL_R) | BIT(0),
 			     CVI_SDHCI_VENDOR_MSHC_CTRL_R);
 	} else if (host->index == MMC_TYPE_SD) {
-		if (host->ops && host->ops->get_cd) {
-			int present = host->ops->get_cd(host);
-
-			if (present == 1) {
-				//Voltage switching flow (3.3)
-				//(reg_pwrsw_auto=1, reg_pwrsw_disc=0, reg_pwrsw_vsel=0(3.0v), reg_en_pwrsw=1)
-				mmio_write_32(TOP_BASE + REG_TOP_SD_PWRSW_CTRL, 0x9);
-				cvi_sdio0_pad_function(false);
-				cvi_sdio0_pad_setting(false);
-			} else {
-				//Voltage close flow
-				//(reg_pwrsw_auto=1, reg_pwrsw_disc=1, reg_pwrsw_vsel=1(1.8v), reg_en_pwrsw=0)
-				mmio_write_32(TOP_BASE + REG_TOP_SD_PWRSW_CTRL, 0xE);
-				cvi_sdio0_pad_function(true);
-				cvi_sdio0_pad_setting(true);
-			}
-		}
+		/*
+		 * Do not look at card detect here. Setting up the slot only
+		 * when a card sits in it leaves the other path - pads muxed
+		 * away to XGPIO, power switch off and discharging - in place
+		 * for the whole Linux run, and mainline has no driver for
+		 * either register, so a card inserted later is never seen.
+		 */
+		//Voltage switching flow (3.3)
+		//(reg_pwrsw_auto=1, reg_pwrsw_disc=0, reg_pwrsw_vsel=0(3.0v), reg_en_pwrsw=1)
+		mmio_write_32(TOP_BASE + REG_TOP_SD_PWRSW_CTRL, 0x9);
+		cvi_sdio0_pad_function(false);
+		cvi_sdio0_pad_setting(false);
 	} else if (host->index == MMC_TYPE_SDIO) {
 		cvi_sdio1_pad_setting();
 	} else {
-- 
